00:04:24.670 --> 00:04:27.550
Now, a labyrinth is a walking course

00:04:27.950 --> 00:04:28.750
that

00:04:29.230 --> 00:04:30.430
goes into a center

00:04:31.185 --> 00:04:32.465
by a

00:04:32.785 --> 00:04:33.985
single path,

00:04:34.305 --> 00:04:36.145
which is rather mysterious.

00:04:36.145 --> 00:04:39.105
It is not logical. It's not a spiral,

00:04:39.345 --> 00:04:41.265
but it is a prescribed path.

00:04:42.305 --> 00:04:43.985
And it takes you eventually,

00:04:44.980 --> 00:04:50.900
winds you around. I'm not going spend all your time to do that, but it gets you to the center, and then you wind back out the same way.

00:04:51.300 --> 00:04:55.860
So, I have been interested in labyrinths for years. They're all over the place.

00:04:56.260 --> 00:05:03.415
There are some 4,000 in The United States at this point, and many in Connecticut. This one's out in Santa Fe,

00:05:04.375 --> 00:05:06.055
and this one's in Ohio.

00:05:07.815 --> 00:05:09.495
Here's one in Albuquerque.

00:05:09.495 --> 00:05:15.175
They can be made of all sorts of things. And this is the one in my yard that I made back in 2014,

00:05:16.190 --> 00:05:21.070
and we use it every day. It's just a pleasure to walk

00:05:22.030 --> 00:05:24.670
in all seasons, and children love it.

00:05:25.870 --> 00:05:27.870
Whenever children come, they want to

00:05:28.190 --> 00:05:29.790
walk it. Grown ups like it.

00:05:31.305 --> 00:05:33.785
Nice in the fall, it's nice in the winter,

00:05:34.585 --> 00:05:35.785
even at night.

00:05:37.945 --> 00:05:41.545
George and I have been to lots of labyrinths. This one is in Massachusetts,

00:05:42.985 --> 00:05:44.665
it's near Plymouth. It's absolutely beautiful.

00:05:45.540 --> 00:05:52.420
And there's a whole book called chasing labyrinths a field guide to labyrinths of Connecticut and Rhode island, meaning, this is a thing.

00:05:53.300 --> 00:05:56.980
One here that they show on the cover is in West Hartford

00:05:57.735 --> 00:06:00.615
